/-!
# The linear program and its dual — Sections 3.2–3.4 and 4.1

Paper: Buzet–Chailloux, *Fine-Grained Unambiguous Measurements*,
arXiv:2510.07298v2 (4 Nov 2025).

This file states Theorem 2 (p. 6 and p. 17), Theorem 3 (p. 21),
Proposition 12 (p. 23), Proposition 13 (p. 23), Proposition 14 (p. 24),
Proposition 15 (p. 25) and Proposition 22 (p. 26) of the paper in the
vocabulary of `MainResults.Definitions.UnambigMeasModel`.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false
noncomputable section

namespace Qatlib.Papers.UnambigMeas25.MainResults.Dual

open Matrix BigOperators
open scoped Classical MatrixOrder ComplexOrder BigOperators

/-- The symmetric family generated by an amplitude vector `α`.  This is used to
state Proposition 14 (density argument), where a new symmetric family is
constructed from perturbed amplitudes. -/
def mkSym {n : ℕ} (α : StateVec n) : SymFamily n where
  α := α
  ψ := fun x => shift x α
  shifted := fun _ => rfl

/-! ### Theorem 2 and Theorem 3 -/

/-- **Theorem 2** (arXiv:2510.07298v2, p. 6, restated p. 17).  Let `S` have full
dual support and let `{F_{H,y}} ∈ Γ(S)`.  For every `k`, `H ∈ Λ_k`, `y ∈ F₂^k`
and all `i, j` in the same dual coset `D_H(s)` (equivalently `H i = H j`),
`|α̂_i|² ⟨b_i|F_{H,y}|b_i⟩ = |α̂_j|² ⟨b_j|F_{H,y}|b_j⟩`.

Source: Theorem 2, p. 6 and p. 17.  Proof route: spectral decomposition of
`F_{H,y}` (Proposition 10), the orthogonal basis of `W_{H,y}` given by
Proposition 11, and the fact that `|⟨b_i|A_s^{H,y}⟩|² = |α̂_i|²` for `i ∈ D_H(s)`
and `0` otherwise.  The paper's `D_H(s)` is re-indexed here by the fibre of
`H.mulVec`; on a fibre the identity reads `|α̂_i|²⟨b_i|F|b_i⟩ = |α̂_j|²⟨b_j|F|b_j⟩`. -/
def theorem2 {n : ℕ} (S : SymFamily n) (hfull : FullDualSupport S)
    (M : Measurement n) (hM : Gamma S M) (k : ℕ) (H : Lam n k)
    (y : Fin k → ZMod 2) (i j : F2 n) (hij : SameDualCoset H i j) : Prop :=
  Complex.normSq (S.hat i) * fourierDiag (M.w k H y) i =
      Complex.normSq (S.hat j) * fourierDiag (M.w k H y) j
  -- Paper: arXiv:2510.07298v2, Theorem 2, p. 6 / p. 17.
  -- Key steps: by Proposition 10, F_{H,y} = ∑_t μ_t |B_t⟩⟨B_t| with |B_t⟩ ∈ W_{H,y};
  -- expand |B_t⟩ in the Proposition 11 basis, |B_t⟩ = ∑_s γ_{t,s}|A_s^{H,y}⟩;
  -- then |⟨b_i|B_t⟩|² = |γ_{t,s}|²|⟨b_i|A_s^{H,y}⟩|² and the last factor is |α̂_i|²
  -- when i ∈ D_H(s) and 0 otherwise.  Summing over t gives the claim.
  -- Gap: Propositions 10 and 11 are themselves not proved; there is no lemma
  -- computing ⟨b_i|A_s⟩ = α̂_i·1[i ∈ D_H(s)].
  -- Suggested tactic: derive `A_s_coeff` as a standalone lemma from the definition
  -- of `AHVec` and character orthogonality, then rewrite under the sum over the
  -- spectral decomposition.

/-- **Theorem 3** (arXiv:2510.07298v2, p. 21).  For every set of symmetric
states `S`, `ρ(S) = ρ^L(S)`: the maximal fine-grained unambiguous score is the
value of the linear program of Definition 6.

Source: Theorem 3, p. 21.  Proof: Theorems 1+2 give `ρ(S) ≤ ρ^L(S)` under full
dual support (Propositions 12), Proposition 13 gives `ρ^L(S) ≤ ρ(S)` under full
dual support, and Proposition 14 removes the full-support assumption by a
density argument. -/
def theorem3 {n : ℕ} (S : SymFamily n) (C : ℕ → ℝ) (hC : ∀ k, 0 ≤ C k) : Prop :=
  rho S C = rhoL S C
  -- Paper: arXiv:2510.07298v2, Theorem 3, p. 21.
  -- Key steps: ρ ≤ ρ^L is Proposition 12 (Theorem 2 inserted into Theorem 1);
  -- ρ^L ≤ ρ is Proposition 13 (explicit measurement built from λ);
  -- density perturbation is Proposition 14.
  -- Gap: Propositions 12, 13, 14 not yet available, and `rho`/`rhoL` are `sSup`
  -- of value sets rather than attained maxima, so the density argument must be
  -- rephrased at the level of `sSup`.
  -- Suggested tactic: `le_antisymm`; transfer the three propositions through
  -- `le_csSup` / `csSup_le` with boundedness side goals.

/-! ### Propositions 12 and 13 -/

/-- **Proposition 12** (arXiv:2510.07298v2, p. 23).  For `S` with full dual
support, `ρ(S) ≤ ρ^L(S)`.

Source: Proposition 12, p. 23.  Proof: start from a maximiser `{F_{H,y}} ∈ Γ_s(S)`
of the semidefinite program, use Theorem 2 to produce the primal variables
`λ_i^{H,y} = |α̂_i|²⟨b_i|F_{H,y}|b_i⟩ / (2^k C(k))` (for the relevant `k`) and
check Equations (1) and (2). -/
def proposition12 {n : ℕ} (S : SymFamily n) (C : ℕ → ℝ) (hC : ∀ k, 0 ≤ C k)
    (hfull : FullDualSupport S) : Prop :=
  rho S C ≤ rhoL S C
  -- Paper: arXiv:2510.07298v2, Proposition 12, p. 23.
  -- Key steps: take {F} ∈ Γ_s(S) maximising (Theorem 1); define
  -- λ_i^{H,y} from the diagonal entries of F_{H,y}; Theorem 2 makes λ constant
  -- on dual cosets, giving Constraint (2); completeness of the POVM gives (1).
  -- Gap: no maximiser existence for `rho` (it is an `sSup`), no construction
  -- of `PrimalFamily` from a `Measurement`.
  -- Suggested tactic: for each candidate measurement build `lam` explicitly and
  -- show `lambdaValue = thm1Expr`; then `csSup_le`.

/-- **Proposition 13** (arXiv:2510.07298v2, p. 23).  For `S` with full dual
support, `ρ^L(S) ≤ ρ(S)`.

Source: Proposition 13, p. 23.  Proof: from the optimal `λ_i^H` construct
one-dimensional operators `F_{H,y} = c_{H,y} |A^{H,y}⟩⟨A^{H,y}|` following the
basis of Proposition 11; the resulting `{F_{H,y}}` is a symmetric fine-grained
unambiguous POVM with `ρ₂ = ρ^L`, and Theorem 1 converts this into `ρ ≥ ρ^L`. -/
def proposition13 {n : ℕ} (S : SymFamily n) (C : ℕ → ℝ) (hC : ∀ k, 0 ≤ C k)
    (hfull : FullDualSupport S) : Prop :=
  rhoL S C ≤ rho S C
  -- Paper: arXiv:2510.07298v2, Proposition 13, p. 23.
  -- Key steps: define F_{H,y} from λ and the basis vectors |A_s^{H,y}⟩; verify
  -- PosSemidef, completeness (using Equation (1)), unambiguity (Proposition 11
  -- orthogonality) and symmetry; compute ρ₂ = ρ^L and apply Theorem 1.
  -- Gap: same as Proposition 12 plus the POVM verification, which needs the
  -- Propositions 10/11 basis algebra.
  -- Suggested tactic: build `Measurement` with `w k H y := (c : ℂ) • outer (AHVec ...)`;
  -- prove `GammaS` field by field and finish with `le_csSup`.

/-! ### Proposition 14 (density) -/

/-- **Proposition 14** (arXiv:2510.07298v2, p. 24).  For every symmetric family
`S` and every `ε > 0` there is a symmetric family `S'` with full dual support
whose `ρ` and `ρ^L` values differ from those of `S` by at most `ε`.

Source: Proposition 14, p. 24.  Proof: perturb the amplitudes,
`|ψ'_x⟩ = √(1−δ)|ψ_x⟩ + √(δ/|T|) ∑_{i∈T}(−1)^{i·x}|b_i⟩`, where `T` is the
zero set of `α̂`; this keeps symmetry and makes all dual coefficients nonzero.
The bound is by continuity of `ρ` and `ρ^L` in the amplitudes. -/
def proposition14 {n : ℕ} (S : SymFamily n) (C : ℕ → ℝ) (hC : ∀ k, 0 ≤ C k)
    (ε : ℝ) (hε : 0 < ε) : Prop :=
  ∃ α' : StateVec n,
      FullDualSupport (mkSym α') ∧
        |rho (mkSym α') C - rho S C| ≤ ε ∧
        |rhoL (mkSym α') C - rhoL S C| ≤ ε
  -- Paper: arXiv:2510.07298v2, Proposition 14, p. 24.
  -- Key steps: define δ = min{ε/(ρ^L(S)+1), ε/(ρ(S)+1), ε/C_max, 1}; set
  -- α̂'_i = √(1−δ) α̂_i for i ∉ T and √(δ/|T|) for i ∈ T (up to a global phase);
  -- show full dual support and the two Lipschitz bounds.
  -- Gap: no continuity/Lipschitz estimates for `rho` and `rhoL` in the model,
  -- and `mkSym` only captures amplitude perturbations with `ψ x = shift x α`.
  -- Suggested tactic: choose `α' := fun j => Real.sqrt (1-δ) * S.α j` plus the
  -- uniform correction, prove `FullDualSupport` via `fourierCoeffM` linearity,
  -- then bound `|rho ...|` by a smallness estimate on the objective.
  -- Candidate dependencies: `ContinuousAt` of finite sums, `abs_sub_le_iff`.

/-! ### Proposition 15 (strong duality) -/

/-- **Proposition 15** (arXiv:2510.07298v2, p. 25).  Strong duality:
`ρ^L(S) = σ^L(S)`.

Source: Proposition 15, p. 25.  Proof: the primal and dual programs are a pair
of finite-dimensional real linear programs in standard form; weak duality plus
the explicit feasible pair `λ = 0` and `b_i = 2^n max_k C(k)` shows both
optima exist and coincide (LP strong duality). -/
def proposition15 {n : ℕ} (S : SymFamily n) (C : ℕ → ℝ) (hC : ∀ k, 0 ≤ C k) : Prop :=
  rhoL S C = sigmaL S C
  -- Paper: arXiv:2510.07298v2, Proposition 15, p. 25.
  -- Key steps: weak duality `sigmaValue ≥ lambdaValue` for every feasible pair;
  -- the primal `λ = 0` is feasible; the dual `b_i = 2^n max_k C(k)` is feasible;
  -- invoke finite-dimensional LP strong duality.
  -- Gap: Mathlib has no ready-made LP strong duality theorem in this form; the
  -- programs here are `sSup`/`sInf` of value sets, so attainment must be shown.
  -- Suggested tactic: prove weak duality by summing Equation (2) over `i ∈ D_H(s)`
  -- and using Constraint (3); then use a separating-hyperplane argument
  -- (`exists_le_of_forall_lt` / Farkas) or an explicit complementary-slackness
  -- construction.
  -- Candidate dependencies: `Real.exists_max_image` (compactness after bounding),
  -- `sSup_eq_sInf`-style duality lemmas—none available; a bespoke LP duality
  -- lemma is needed.

/-! ### Proposition 22 (affine invariance of dual solutions) -/

/-- An affine bijection of `𝔽₂ⁿ`, i.e. `x ↦ P x + v` with `P` invertible. -/
def IsAffineEquiv {n : ℕ} (f : F2 n → F2 n) : Prop :=
  ∃ (P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) (ZMod 2)) (v : F2 n) (g : F2 n → F2 n),
    P.det ≠ 0 ∧ (∀ x : F2 n, f x = P.mulVec x + v) ∧
      (∀ x : F2 n, g (f x) = x) ∧ (∀ x : F2 n, f (g x) = x)

/-- **Proposition 22** (arXiv:2510.07298v2, p. 26).  If `b` is a dual solution,
then for every affine bijection `f(x) = Px + v` (`P ∈ GL_n(𝔽₂)`), the permuted
family `b_{f(i)}` is again a dual solution.

Source: Proposition 22, p. 26 (proof deferred to Appendix A).  Proof: `f` maps
dual cosets to dual cosets (affine subspaces of the same dimension), and
`∑_{i∈D} b_{f(i)} = ∑_{j∈f(D)} b_j ≥ C(k)2^k`; nonnegativity is immediate. -/
def proposition22 {n : ℕ} (C : ℕ → ℝ) (b : F2 n → ℝ)
    (hb : DualFeasible C b) (f : F2 n → F2 n) (hf : IsAffineEquiv f) : Prop :=
  DualFeasible C fun i => b (f i)
  -- Paper: arXiv:2510.07298v2, Proposition 22, p. 26; proof in Appendix A.
  -- Key steps: nonnegativity is pointwise; for the coset constraint, change
  -- variables j = f(i) and observe that f(D_H(s)) is again a fibre
  -- {j | H'(j) = s'} of the full-rank matrix H' = H P^{-1}, with s' absorbing v;
  -- then apply feasibility of b to that fibre.
  -- Gap: no proof that an affine bijection maps `matVec`-fibres to `matVec`-fibres
  -- of another full-rank matrix; the inverse `g` must be used to rewrite the sum.
  -- Suggested tactic: `unfold DualFeasible cosetSum`; `refine ⟨?_, ?_⟩`;
  -- use `Finset.sum_bij'` with `f`/`g`, and construct `H' : Lam n k` from
  -- `H.1 * P⁻¹` using `Matrix.rank_mul_eq_right_of_isUnit_det`.
  -- Candidate dependencies: `Matrix.rank_mul_eq_right_of_isUnit_det`,
  -- `Matrix.det_ne_zero_iff_isUnit`, `Finset.sum_bij'`.

end Qatlib.Papers.UnambigMeas25.MainResults.Dual
